#: Implementation.md:55
msgid ""
"The simplest kernel for calculating the coordination in a group of atoms is:"
msgstr ""

#: Implementation.md:134
msgid ""
"You can view this kernel as the inner body of the nested for loop that can "
"be used to write the most naive serial implementation of the coordination "
"(the outer loop would run on the `i` variable)."
msgstr ""

#: Implementation.md:138
msgid ""
"The use of global memory should be limited in a kernel, because accessing "
"global memory is far slower than working on shared memory (that it is not "
"used in this case, shared memory is shared between kernels within a group) "
"or the variables, (the \"local memory\") declared within each kernel."
msgstr ""

#: Implementation.md:143
msgid ""
"Here within each kernel, I have accumulated the values in local variables "
"and not on the global memory and then update the global memory only at the "
"end of the calculation. Using `mycoord += coord;` instead of `ncoordOut[i] "
"+= coord;` within the loop will speed up the calculations."
msgstr ""

#: Implementation.md:148
msgid ""
"In the actual implementation the `float myVirial[9];` and `float d[3];` are "
"presented as a collection of 9 and 3 single declared values to use the "
"registers of the kernels, here are declared as an array for clarity."
msgstr ""

#: Implementation.md:154
msgid ""
"The switching parameters are stored in a simple struct: when called, kernels"
" accept struct variables as inputs, making it simpler to pass parameters to "
"functions:"
msgstr ""

